Slide 3 – Storing Opt-ins
Objective
"In this section, I'll explain how member opt-in information is stored and maintained in our system after the member has provided consent."
What is an Opt-in?
An opt-in means a member has given permission to receive SMS messages from Optum regarding healthcare programs such as preventive screenings, annual wellness visits, or medication reminders.
Once the member provides consent, that information needs to be stored securely so that future campaigns know which members can legally receive text messages.
Why do we store Opt-ins?
There are several reasons:
Maintain the member's consent status.
Ensure compliance with SMS regulations.
Prevent sending messages to members who have not consented.
Provide an audit trail showing when and how consent was obtained.
What information is stored?
Typically, the system stores information such as:
Field	Description
Member ID	Unique member identifier
Phone Number	Mobile number used for SMS
Opt-in Status	Yes/No
Opt-in Date	Date consent was received
Opt-in Source	Email, Web Form, etc.
Created Date	Record creation timestamp
Updated Date	Last modified timestamp
Where is it stored?
Technically, the opt-in information is stored in a Snowflake table.
For example:
SMS_OPT_IN
The table acts as the system of record for member consent.
Every campaign references this table before generating the final member population.
How is it used?
The flow is:
Member Opts In
        ↓
Opt-in Record Stored
        ↓
Monthly Population Generation
        ↓
System Checks Opt-in Status
        ↓
Eligible Members Selected
Only members with a valid opt-in are included in SMS campaigns.
Data Maintenance
As part of our process:
New opt-ins are inserted into the table.
Existing records can be updated if the member changes their phone number or consent status.
Audit columns capture who updated the record and when.
This ensures the data remains accurate and traceable.
Importance
Maintaining accurate opt-in data helps us:
Comply with regulations.
Avoid sending unwanted messages.
Improve campaign accuracy.
Support reporting and audits.
Closing
"So in summary, this table serves as the central repository for member SMS consent. Every monthly campaign relies on this data to determine which members are eligible to receive text messages."
If someone asks a technical question
Q: Why can't we simply use the phone number?
Answer:
Having a phone number alone does not mean we have permission to text that member. The opt-in status determines whether the member has provided consent to receive SMS communications.
Q: When is this table updated?
Answer:
The table is updated whenever a new consent is received or an existing consent status changes. The exact source depends on the business process, such as a web form or other approved intake methods.
Q: What happens if a member opts out later?
Answer:
The opt-out is processed separately and stored in the opt-out table. During population generation, both the opt-in and opt-out information are checked so that members who have opted out are excluded from future campaigns.
